FIVE TERMINAL ASSUMPTIONS, ASSESSED BEFORE FREEZE
positive: complete drain permits natural producer 0/null and consumer 0/null.
producer-exit7: complete drain permits natural producer 7/null; STREAM_PROCESS
must encode that exact terminal outcome and remain the forwarded failure.
consumer-failure: unchanged v3.1 caller identity/token/actual stdout errorMonitor
and uncaughtExceptionMonitor predicate; its narrowly proven EPIPE alternative
is NOT granted to any other control. The original 28 synthetic cases are copied
byte-identically. No global producer error handling changes.
timeout: throwing in the blocked consume callback closes the async iterator's
pipe before core catch signals the producer. That race cannot justify SIGTERM.
allocation-mutant: its controlled abort has the same ordering race. It also
requires a real touched allocation boundary and an actual supervisor kill.

ORDERING DELTA ONLY FOR TWO CONTROLLED ABORTS
The overlay captures the actual ChildProcess through a worker-local spawn hook,
as the existing consumer observer already does, without modifying core.mjs.
While consumption remains blocked and stdout is owned/open, send SIGTERM through
that child handle. Await exit (Node has closed its process handle at exit), THEN
destroy the owned stdout, await child close, then throw the original boundary
error object. Record all stages plus actual exit/close status and object identity.
Do not await child close while leaving already-touched unread stdout open: close
depends on stdio closure and can deadlock. The chosen sequence waits process exit
first, not a PID-absence polling loop, then completes pipe cleanup before throw.
The pinned Node internal/child_process source hash is in PREFLIGHT.json.
No guarantee against hostile external process intervention is invented. Wrong
terminal results still fail strictly. Producer SIGTERM receipt alone is not proof:
actual ChildProcess exit/close and core.process must match. Errors/timeouts in
ordered cleanup are unsafe STOP, never semantic waivers.

LIMITS AND BOUNDARIES
All PIN.policy values, fixture and complete core bytes remain unchanged. Consumer
absolute CURRENT sampled RSS must remain strictly below 268435456 for positive;
no baseline subtraction, no threshold increase. Producer/supervisor metrics stay
separate. Consumer heap flag stays --max-old-space-size=96. Fixture remains
1073872896 bytes, 16386 writes; negative controls consume a proper prefix.
Allocation retains touched 8388608-byte Buffers, one supervised allocation at a
time, at most 40. At >=268435456, stop further allocation; ordered SIGTERM/reap of
producer precedes exact V3_RSS_LIMIT and durable consumer receipt; supervisor then
actually SIGTERMs the still-owned consumer. The existing overshoot ceiling remains.
Both controlled stop stages share the existing 3000ms cleanup grace. Overall
supervisor control deadline remains 45000ms; existing TERM then KILL grace is now
followed by an explicit terminal bound at deadline + two cleanup graces. No
unbounded await of child close remains in the supervisor. Native ps probes have
their existing bounded 10000ms calls and may delay timer dispatch; this is not a
hard real-time guarantee. Missing final closure proof is an unsafe STOP even if
later cleanup happens. No post-exit signal targets a stale recorded leader PID;
group emergency signals require a still-owned non-exited child leader and record
current group membership. Lost ownership is reported unsafe, never assumed reaped.

AGGREGATION DELTA
Persist raw receipts before assertions. Then independently authenticate the
frozen recipe/tool/history bindings and all 78 live v3.1 files; require valid
numeric receipts, observed worker exit/close, core's producer-close result,
both observed PIDs absent, empty process group, completed owned cleanup.
Only after this safety proof can an ordinary semantic assertion failure permit
the NEXT independent control. Such failure permanently keeps aggregate exit 1.
Cleanup/integrity/missing proof stops the cohort. All five real controls are
planned in one invocation; allocation is not hidden behind ordinary assertion
failure. Failed predicates are neither rerun nor rescored.
Ordinary synthetic/forwarding expectation failures also keep aggregate exit1
without suppressing safe real controls; forwarding physical safety failure stops.

FROZEN FORWARDING CONTROLS
Six small actual supervised cohorts use the same aggregation/safety functions:
ordinary failure followed by marker after reap; TERM-ignoring child forced KILL
then unsafe cleanup stop; private test-binding corruption stops marker; missing
receipt stops; reap-proof omission after actual successful reap stops; exact
reason loss despite exit0 fails while later marker runs and aggregate remains1.
These create eight actual small child executions if all expectations hold.
They are not the five stream controls. Their physical reap/recipe integrity must
hold even when a test deliberately invalidates its private proof. A real physical
cleanup or real recipe integrity failure stops subsequent controls.
Eight constructed ordered-predicate controls retain two strict accepts and reject
six failures: lost timeout/RSS reason, wrong timeout/RSS terminal, lost identity,
and early pipe close. These are synthetic, not actual producer executions.
All original 28 consumer terminal controls remain byte-identical (2 accept,
26 reject); their observed outcomes are not adapted.

